      "dom_id": "question-154080",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The shoulder height of a moose $h$ measures $2.1\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest tenth of a meter. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement.",
      "worked_solution": "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
